= 1
{parent=Paper 6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

First consider finite-dimensional <Lie algebra representations>. The operators representing an <abelian Lie algebra> commute. They have a common <eigenvector>: take an <eigenspace> for the first operator of a <basis> of the algebra, observe that every remaining operator preserves it, and repeat inside this nonzero space. The resulting line is an <invariant subspace>, so an <irreducible representation> must be one-dimensional. Its action has the form $xv=\lambda(x)v$ for a <linear functional> $\lambda$. Conversely, every such functional defines a <Lie algebra representation>, since both the <Lie brackets> in the algebra and the <commutators> of its scalar operators vanish. Distinct functionals give nonisomorphic representations. Thus **the irreducibles are precisely the characters $\lambda\in\mathfrak g^*$**.

Even without a finite-dimensional assumption on the module, this conclusion for a finite-dimensional <abelian Lie algebra> follows from the <weak Hilbert Nullstellensatz>: its <universal enveloping algebra> is the <polynomial> algebra $\operatorname{Sym}(\mathfrak g)$. A simple module over a commutative algebra is its quotient by a <maximal ideal>, and every <maximal ideal> of this <polynomial> algebra over $\mathbb C$ has residue field $\mathbb C$.

**Indecomposable does not imply irreducible.** For the one-dimensional <abelian Lie algebra> $\mathbb Cx$, let $x$ act on $\mathbb C^2$ by the <nilpotent Jordan block>
$$
N=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The line spanned by the first coordinate vector is an <invariant subspace>, so the module is reducible. If it were a <direct sum> of two nonzero submodules, both would have <dimension> one; nilpotence would force $N$ to act as zero on both, contrary to $N\ne0$. Thus it is an <indecomposable representation>.

For a finite-dimensional module $V$, put $d=\dim V$ and define its <generalized weight spaces> intrinsically by
$$
V^\lambda=\{v\in V:(\rho(x)-\lambda(x)I)^d v=0\text{ for every }x\in\mathfrak g\}.
$$
To obtain the decomposition, choose a <basis> $x_1,\ldots,x_r$ of $\mathfrak g$, split into <generalized eigenspaces> of $\rho(x_1)$, and successively split each piece by $\rho(x_2),\ldots,\rho(x_r)$. All the pieces are submodules because the acting operators commute. On one resulting piece, $\rho(x_j)$ has a unique <eigenvalue> $\lambda_j$. The commuting operators can be simultaneously upper triangularized by repeating the common-eigenvector argument on quotients. Hence on this piece every $\rho(x_j)-\lambda_j I$ is strictly upper triangular. Any <linear combination> is also strictly upper triangular, and its $d$th power vanishes. Extending $\lambda_j$ linearly therefore identifies the piece with $V^\lambda$. Different tuples give disjoint pieces; if a component has a different tuple from $\lambda$, at least one $\rho(x_j)-\lambda(x_j)I$ is invertible there. Consequently
$$
\boxed{V=\bigoplus_{\lambda\in\mathfrak g^*}V^\lambda,}
$$
with only finitely many nonzero summands. Since the definition uses every $x$ rather than a chosen <basis>, this <generalized-weight decomposition for a nilpotent Lie algebra> is canonical. Its summands need not be irreducible, as the <Jordan block> example already shows.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <derived series of a Lie algebra> is
$$
\mathfrak b^{(0)}=\mathfrak b,\qquad \mathfrak b^{(j+1)}=[\mathfrak b^{(j)},\mathfrak b^{(j)}].
$$
The algebra is a <solvable Lie algebra> if $\mathfrak b^{(m)}=0$ for some $m$. Here a bracket of subspaces means the <linear span> of their <Lie brackets>.

The <Lie theorem> states that a nonzero finite-dimensional representation of a complex <solvable Lie algebra> has a common <eigenvector>; equivalently, its operators can be simultaneously upper triangularized. Applied to an <irreducible Lie algebra representation>, the common <eigenvector> gives a nonzero invariant line, which must be the entire module. A scalar action $\rho(x)=\lambda(x)$ respects the <Lie bracket> exactly when $\lambda([x,y])=0$. Thus, in the finite-dimensional setting,
$$
\boxed{\text{irreducibles are }\mathbb C_\lambda,\qquad \lambda\in(\mathfrak b/[\mathfrak b,\mathfrak b])^*.}
$$
Every functional displayed gives a one-dimensional irreducible, and two such modules are isomorphic exactly when their functionals agree.

The finite-dimensional qualification is essential here, although the printed wording does not repeat it. For example, let $[x,y]=y$ and act on $\mathbb C[t]$ by $xf(t)=tf(t)$ and $yf(t)=f(t-1)$. These operators satisfy $[x,y]f=yf$. A nonzero submodule stable under $x$ is an <ideal> $p(t)\mathbb C[t]$. Stability under $y$ forces $p(t)\mid p(t-1)$, so the two <polynomials>, having the same degree and leading coefficient, are equal. In characteristic zero a <polynomial> invariant under a nonzero shift is constant. The module is therefore an <infinite-dimensional simple module for the two-dimensional affine Lie algebra>. It rules out extending the one-dimensional classification to unrestricted representations.

= 2
{parent=Paper 6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Let $B$ be the given <invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra>. Choose a <basis> $x_1,\ldots,x_d$ and its $B$-dual <basis> $x^1,\ldots,x^d$, so $B(x_i,x^j)=\delta_i^j$. The <Casimir element> is
$$
\boxed{\Omega_B=\sum_i x_i x^i\in U(\mathfrak g).}
$$
The tensor $\sum_i x_i\otimes x^i$ is the inverse tensor of $B$, so it is independent of the chosen <basis>. Multiplication in the <universal enveloping algebra> therefore makes $\Omega_B$ independent of that choice as well.

For $z\in\mathfrak g$, write $[z,x_i]=\sum_j a_{ji}x_j$. Invariance means $B([z,x],y)+B(x,[z,y])=0$, whence $[z,x^i]=-\sum_j a_{ij}x^j$. Therefore
$$
[z,\Omega_B]=\sum_{i,j}a_{ji}x_jx^i-\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}x_ix^j=0,
$$
by interchanging $i,j$ in the second sum. Since $\mathfrak g$ generates its <universal enveloping algebra>, $\Omega_B$ is central. This argument does not require adding a symmetry assumption to the given <bilinear form>.

For the <sl2 Lie algebra>, take
$$
e=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad f=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad h=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad B(X,Y)=\operatorname{tr}(XY).
$$
The cyclic identity for the <trace> gives $B([Z,X],Y)+B(X,[Z,Y])=0$. The form is <nondegenerate>, since $B(e,f)=1$, $B(h,h)=2$, and all the other pairings except $B(f,e)$ are zero. The dual of $(e,f,h)$ is $(f,e,h/2)$, so
$$
\Omega_B=ef+fe+\tfrac12h^2.
$$
By the <classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations>, an irreducible of <dimension> $n$ has a <highest-weight vector> $v$ with $ev=0$ and $hv=mv$, where $m=n-1$. Since $ef=fe+h$, one obtains
$$
\Omega_Bv=\left(m+\tfrac12m^2\right)v.
$$
Centrality and the <Schur lemma> then give the answer on the entire representation:
$$
\boxed{\Omega_B=\frac{n^2-1}{2}I.}
$$
This <Casimir eigenvalue for sl2> depends on normalization. Choosing the <Killing form> instead, $K=4B$, scales the dual <basis> and the <Casimir element> by $1/4$, giving $(n^2-1)I/8$.

